 "productionNotes": "No production figure for the 250 Testa Rossa is agreed on by the sources fetched for this page, and the disagreements are about scope rather than counting. Sports Car Market, in its profile of chassis 0714 TR, states that 34 250 Testa Rossas were built including prototypes and the 330 TRI/LM, and its profile of 0666 TR repeats 34 cars built from 1957 until 1962; the supercars.net guide also says 34 units of the 250 TR and its variants. Wikipedia and classic.com both put the figure at 33 of all types between 1957 and 1962, with 19 customer cars sold to independent teams, and autoevolution also says 33. The difference is most likely whether the 1962 330 TRI/LM, a four-liter car on a Testa Rossa chassis, is counted, but no source consulted here says so explicitly, so productionTotal is left null. The pontoon-fender count is a second, separate disagreement: Sports Car Market says 0714 TR is one of only 21 cars with pontoon fenders, while Autoblog and conceptcarz, both reporting the 2024 RM Sotheby's sealed sale of 0738 TR, say the design is present in only 19 cars, which matches the 19 customer cars in Wikipedia and classic.com. Whether the two prototypes and the early works cars are included is the likely explanation; again, not stated by any source. What is not in dispute is the shape of the run: two 1957 prototypes, a batch of customer cars built through 1958 for privateers, and a small number of works cars each year through 1961 that were rebodied, rebuilt and renumbered as the factory saw fit, which is exactly why a single clean number does not exist. Sports Car Market lists the original price as approximately $12,000; that figure appears in two Sports Car Market profiles and nowhere else in this research, so it is a single-publisher figure.",
